{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 3771 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "1bf709292e275e75e4a72689d7034e32c135106406d7770867f6f190178e8c97" :edb/db-before-t 3770 :edb/db-after-t 3771 :edb/tx-data [[13194139537083 50 #inst "2026-10-18T03:57:00.526Z" 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186134504 1071 "![](images/a2/a266f4d3ab4254684c91cb422f96b70a4c9973f587b6afb2cd7af5db45e86561.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $y=0$, $y=2$, $z=0,$ and $z=4-x-y.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4-x-y$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=2$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186134504 1071 "![](images/a2/a266f4d3ab4254684c91cb422f96b70a4c9973f587b6afb2cd7af5db45e86561.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $y=0$, $y=2$, $z=0,$ and $z=4-x-y.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186134504 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(x,y)=1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4-x-y$” and statement “${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=2$” are false. We have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=4-x-y.$\n\n![](images/cd/cd218d4c4ef62d63ae2954c0974fb25a95257d9fc322834b0dec4aae3e8e99fc.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186134504 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II and statement III are false. We have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=4-x-y.$\n\n![](images/cd/cd218d4c4ef62d63ae2954c0974fb25a95257d9fc322834b0dec4aae3e8e99fc.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186148204 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+25y=17\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nII. $y=A{e}^{-3t}+B{e}^{4t}+17\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+17\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nIII. $y=R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186148204 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+25y=17\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186148204 1074 "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+25y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{2}{3}.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly $y$ $={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)$ $-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ $+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y$ $=R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$ $-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ $+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is $y$ $={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)$ $-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ $+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$; $y$ $=R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$ $-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ $+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ only." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186148204 1074 "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+25y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{2}{3}.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly I and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186152632 1071 "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186152632 1071 "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n- **I.** $x{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$\n- **II.** ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=10$\n- **III.** ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′}+16y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=0$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186152825 1071 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{2}-6$ and $y=2-{x}^{2}.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-6,6]$\n- **II.** ${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-6,6]$\n- **III.** ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186152825 1071 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{2}-6$ and $y=2-{x}^{2}.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186152825 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/76/760595b548b2327b9c7c44f336d2c0c2b6a69688c887587f162f8ff2ac5bb5ab.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186152825 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/76/760595b548b2327b9c7c44f336d2c0c2b6a69688c887587f162f8ff2ac5bb5ab.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-6,6]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-6,6]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-6,6]$” only." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186152872 1071 "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nI. $(x-1)^{2}{y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y=0$\nII. $8{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=10$\nIII. ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+1,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=5$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186152872 1071 "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186157779 1071 "Let $n\\in Z.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $n>2,$ then $\\underline{\\hspace{3em}}.$\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is trivial?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186157779 1071 "Let $n\\in Z.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $n>2,$ then $______.$\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is trivial?\n- **I.** $n=0$\n- **II.** $0>1$\n- **III.** ${n}^{2}\\ge 0$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186157779 1074 "We say that an implication has a *trivial proof* if the consequent is always true:\n\n$$\nP\\,⇒\\overbrace{Q}^{\\text{Always true}}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our consequents in turn.\n\n- Predicate $n=0$, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is *not* always true. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement $0>1$ is always false. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Predicate ${n}^{2}\\ge 0$, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is always true. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${n}^{2}\\ge 0$ only." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186157779 1074 "We say that an implication has a *trivial proof* if the consequent is always true:\n\n$$\nP\\,⇒\\overbrace{Q}^{\\text{Always true}}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our consequents in turn.\n\n- Predicate I, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is *not* always true. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement II is always false. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is always true. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186157781 1071 "Let $n\\in Z.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $\\underline{\\hspace{3em}},$ then $n>0.$\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is vacuous?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186157781 1071 "Let $n\\in Z.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $______,$ then $n>0.$\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is vacuous?\n- **I.** $n<0$\n- **II.** $0>1$\n- **III.** ${n}^{2}<0$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186157781 1074 "We say that an implication has a *vacuous proof* if the antecedent is always false: \n\n$$\n\\overbrace{P}^{\\text{Always false}}⇒\\,Q\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the consequent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our antecedents in turn.\n\n- Predicate $n<0$, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is *not* always false. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement $0>1$ is always false. $\\,✓$\n\n- Predicate ${n}^{2}<0$, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is always false. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $0>1$; ${n}^{2}<0$ only." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186157781 1074 "We say that an implication has a *vacuous proof* if the antecedent is always false: \n\n$$\n\\overbrace{P}^{\\text{Always false}}⇒\\,Q\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the consequent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our antecedents in turn.\n\n- Predicate I, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is *not* always false. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement II is always false. $\\,✓$\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is always false. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186162343 1071 "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186162343 1071 "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $P(X=1,Y=1)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n\n- **II.** $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\n- **III.** $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186162343 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ | $0.75$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement $P(X=1,Y=1)$ $=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$ is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.6.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.75\\cdot 0.8=0.6.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement “$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$” is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.05=0.25\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,0) & =0.15=0.75\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(0,1) & =0.20=0.25\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement “$X$ and $Y$ are independent” is true. Since statement “$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$” is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $P(X=1,Y=1)$ $=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$; “$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$”; “$X$ and $Y$ are independent”." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186162343 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=1$ 