l equation ${y}^{′}-x=-7y$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${x}^{4}{y}^{‴}+3y={2}^{y}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,$ and ${y}^{″}(0)=0.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $x{y}^{″}-6y=14$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186253513 1071 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186253513 1071 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}=-7y,\\,y(0)={y}^{′}(0)={y}^{″}(0)=-4$\n- **II.** $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(0)=2$\n- **III.** $6{y}^{″}+xy={e}^{x}$" 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186253513 1074 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}$ $=-7y,$ $\\,y(0)$ $={y}^{′}(0)$ $={y}^{″}(0)$ $=-4$ is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}=-7y$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)={y}^{′}(0)={y}^{″}(0)=-4.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(0)=2$ is a first-order initial value 