** $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- **III.** $X$ and $Y$ are independent\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186275118 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ | $0.7$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ | $0.3$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.35$ | $0.65$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements $P(X=1,Y=1)$ $=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$ and “$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$” are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.05.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.3\\cdot 0.65=0.195.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=1,Y=1)\\ne P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement “$X$ and $Y$ are independent” is false. Since statement “$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$” is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186275118 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ | $0.7$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ | $0.3$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.35$ | $0.65$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.05.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.3\\cdot 0.65=0.195.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=1,Y=1)\\ne P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186278386 1071 "![](images/7b/7bb6d2b9f9e09de44e078657ce3517bfd78261d3206743b7882abf0112f38a93.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $y=0$, $y=1$, $z=0,$ and $z=3-x+y.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=3-x+y$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186278386 1071 "![](images/7b/7bb6d2b9f9e09de44e078657ce3517bfd78261d3206743b7882abf0112f38a93.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $y=0$, $y=1$, $z=0,$ and $z=3-x+y.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186278386 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ is false. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=3-x+y$” is true, while statement “${f}_{1}(x,y)=1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4$” is false. Indeed, we have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=3-x+y.$\n\n![](images/65/65e179e16b66009b9168c4380032b01c3a79b3c4a2afc3b8e5084defb77b85fc.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=3-x+y$” only." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186278386 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Indeed, we have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=3-x+y.$\n\n![](images/65/65e179e16b66009b9168c4380032b01c3a79b3c4a2afc3b8e5084defb77b85fc.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186278389 1071 "![](images/e5/e5369264a9ab9328449bafd6fd3dcda1a70b7d90cbaf4f9815211e0cc3873fc0.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=3$, $y=0$, $y=2$, $z=0,$ and $4x+5z=15.$ Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=\\frac{15-4x}{5}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=3$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186278389 1071 "![](images/e5/e5369264a9ab9328449bafd6fd3dcda1a70b7d90cbaf4f9815211e0cc3873fc0.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=3$, $y=0$, $y=2$, $z=0,$ and $4x+5z=15.$ Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186278389 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(x,y)=2$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=\\frac{15-4x}{5}$” and statement “${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=3$” are false. We have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=\\frac{15-4x}{5}.$\n\n![](images/ed/edea8af334c3362f583fcb86709150690eeec79a54185b67d242bfa14a8e4c75.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186278389 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II and statement III are false. We have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=\\frac{15-4x}{5}.$\n\n![](images/ed/edea8af334c3362f583fcb86709150690eeec79a54185b67d242bfa14a8e4c75.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186278403 1071 "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $z=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=-2x-2y+6.$ Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 4}$\n- **II.** $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2}$\n- **III.** ${f}_{1}(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2x-2y+6$" 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186278403 1071 "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $z=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=-2x-2y+6.$ Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186278403 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\,:\\\\\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ is true, while statement $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2}$ is false. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=-2x-2y+6$ onto $xy$-plane. \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-2y+6 & =8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}-2y & =2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+({y}^{2}-2y+1) & =4 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=4$ centered at $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 4}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2x-2y+6$” is false. We have that $z$ is \n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,y)=-2x-2y+6,$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\,:\\\\\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186278403 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=-2x-2y+6$ onto $xy$-plane. \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-2y+6 & =8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}-2y & =2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+({y}^{2}-2y+1)