l the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-3<x<3,\\\\\\,0<y<|2x|\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$; $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-1<x<1,\\\\\\,1<y<3-{x}^{2}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186278582 1071 "Which of the following sets are open?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0,\\,0<y<\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-2<x<2,\\,0<y<{x}^{2}+1\\}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186278582 1071 "Which of the following sets are open?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186278582 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186278582 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0,\\\\\\,0<y<\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$; $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-2<x<2,\\\\\\,0<y<{x}^{2}+1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186281464 1071 "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2},$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186281464 1071 "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2},$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The zero-polynomial belongs to $U.$\n\n- **II.** For any $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we have $p(x)-q(x)=q(x)-p(x).$\n\n- **III.** There are polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in U$ such that $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)\\ne p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)].$" 13194139