083 17592186576411 false] [17592186284612 1071 "![](images/d2/d294519b3909517b9507322c86ad970550ab388a8d759b0ada99ed18627fc4bd.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $y=0$, $y=2$, $z=0$, $z=1$, $x=0,$ and $2x+y=6.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186284612 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(y,z)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\\\\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $yz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=6-2y$” is false, while statement “${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{6-y}{2}$” is true. We have\n\n- the *back* surface $x={f}_{1}(y,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *front* surface $x={f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{6-y}{2}.$\n\n![](images/48/487b57182e190cbacf3b1ad30f55d227b59fb470e8b5609ad72327d229e53e45.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(y,z)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\\\\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$; “${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{6-y}{2}$” only." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186284612 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the