t P(Y=2)$; “$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$”; “$X$ and $Y$ are independent”." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186383466 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.45$ | $0.75$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=2)=f(1,2)=0.15.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.25\\cdot 0.6=0.15.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.1=0.25\\cdot 0.4={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(2,1) & =0.3=0.75\\cdot 0.4={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(2,2) & =0.45=0.75\\cdot 0.6={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186383473 1071 "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537083 1759218