17592186576411 true] [17592186410575 1071 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186410575 1071 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y\\,i-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=(8x-3y)\\,i+(4y-3x+1)\\,j$" 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186410575 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}$ and $Q=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y$ and $Q=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=8x-3y$ and $Q=4y-3x+1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186410575 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}$ and $Q=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y$ and $Q=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=8x-3y$ and $Q=4y-3x+1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}\\,j$; $H$ $=(8x-3y)\\,i$ $+(4y-3x+1)\\,j$ only." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186410576 1071 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186410576 1071 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2})\\,i+({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7})\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=({y}^{3}-6x)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2})\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j$" 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186410576 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2}$ and $Q={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P={y}^{3}-6x$ and $Q=3x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186410576 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2}$ and $Q={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P={y}^{3}-6x$ and $Q=3x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F$ $=({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2})\\,i$ $+({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7})\\,j$; $G=({y}^{3}-6x)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2})\\,j$; $H=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j$." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186412806 1071 "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186412806 1071 "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n- **I.** $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$\n- **II.** $x(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- **III.** $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186412806 1074 "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option $x(t)$ $=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$ $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multipliers of the first and the second terms are not constant.\n\n- Option $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$; $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ only." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186412806 1074 "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that 