n.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186435739 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<2\\}$ only." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186435740 1071 "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x<y<{x}^{2},\\,1<x<3\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<1\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0<y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 false] [17592186435740 1071 "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537083 17592186576411 true] [17592186435740 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set con